Package: homelab-dnsdist
Architecture: all
Depends: perl (>= 5.36), libyaml-libyaml-perl, homelab-common (>= 0.1.19), dnsdist
Recommends: homelab-agent
Description: dnsdist DNS load-balancer edge for the homelab-* ecosystem
 Ships homelab-dnsdist-apply-backends, which reads an editable
 /etc/homelab/dnsdist/backends.yml (listen address -> one or more pools
 of backend PowerDNS servers) and regenerates /etc/dnsdist/dnsdist.conf
 wholesale, validating with `dnsdist --check-config` before ever
 touching the live config.
 .
 Exists for the same reason homelab-haproxy/homelab-webproxy do --
 decoupling "holds the public IP" from "runs the actual service" -- but
 for DNS specifically, using PowerDNS's own purpose-built edge tool
 rather than raw kernel NAT (see homelab-edge-forward, this package's
 predecessor for this exact use case). Unlike raw DNAT+SNAT, dnsdist:
 .
  * LISTENS directly on the public address (same model as haproxy's
    frontends / webproxy's vhosts) instead of relying on
    DNAT+SNAT+ip_forward -- no kernel-level IP forwarding, no
    conntrack/forward-chain rules to get right.
  * Does real ACTIVE health-checking of each backend with automatic
    failover -- the one thing raw nftables genuinely cannot do.
  * Supports real load-balancing across multiple backend PowerDNS
    instances in a pool (HA, same idea as postfix/dovecot's own HA
    trios elsewhere in this fleet) -- not a substitute for genuine
    multi-nameserver redundancy behind a SECOND public IP, which
    remains DNS's own native mechanism for that (independent NS
    records), not something to build here either.
  * Preserves the real client IP to the backend via PROXY protocol
    (useProxyProtocol=true on every generated backend) -- same
    real-client-IP concern homelab-haproxy's own send-proxy already
    solves for postfix/dovecot. The backend PowerDNS must be
    configured to expect it (proxy-protocol-from) or queries are
    rejected.
